import org.elasticsearch.action.ActionListener;
import org.elasticsearch.cluster.ClusterState;
import org.elasticsearch.xpack.ccr.action.usage.CCRUsageRequest;
import org.elasticsearch.xpack.ccr.action.usage.CCRUsageResponse;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Build the request that will be sent to the master node
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
CCRUsageRequest usageRequest = new CCRUsageRequest();   // populate if needed

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Grab the current cluster state (normally you get this from the
//    ClusterService or via a ClusterStateRequest)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
ClusterState clusterState = /* obtain from your context */;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Create an ActionListener that will receive the CCR usage stats
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
ActionListener<CCRUsageResponse> usageListener = new ActionListener<CCRUsageResponse>() {
    @Override
    public void onResponse(CCRUsageResponse response) {
        // The response contains the number of follower indices and
        // auto‑follow patterns.  Adjust the field names to match the
        // actual API you are using.
        System.out.println("Follower indices count: " + response.getFollowerIndicesCount());
        System.out.println("Auto‑follow patterns count: " + response.getAutoFollowPatternsCount());
    }

    @Override
    public void onFailure(Exception e) {
        System.err.println("Failed to fetch CCR usage stats");
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
};

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Call the helper – note that all arguments are passed as strings
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
functions.CCRUsageTransportAction_masterOperation(
    new java.util.HashMap<String, Object>() {{
        put("task", "masterOperationTask");          // any task id you like
        put("request", usageRequest.toString());     // stringified request
        put("state", clusterState.toString());       // stringified cluster state
        put("listener", usageListener.toString());   // stringified listener
    }}
);
```

### What’s happening under the hood?

| Step | What the helper does |
|------|----------------------|
| **1** | Builds a `CCRUsageRequest` (you can set filters or other options if the API supports them). |
| **2** | Serialises the current `ClusterState` so the master node can evaluate the cluster’s CCR configuration. |
| **3** | Instantiates an `ActionListener<CCRUsageResponse>` that will be invoked once the master node has finished gathering the statistics. |
| **4** | Calls `CCRUsageTransportAction_masterOperation`, which internally forwards the request to the master node, collects the usage data, and then calls `onResponse` or `onFailure` on the listener. |

> **Tip** – If you’re working inside an existing Elasticsearch plugin or node, you can obtain the `ClusterState` directly from the `ClusterService` (`clusterService.state()`) and you’ll already have an `ActionListener` available from the transport layer. In that case you can skip the manual string conversion and pass the objects directly; the helper’s signature is just a convenience wrapper for the Java‑8 SDK.

Feel free to adapt the field names (`getFollowerIndicesCount()`, `getAutoFollowPatternsCount()`) to match the actual getters exposed by your version of `CCRUsageResponse`.

